#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:5
msgid "Xonsh Session"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:7
msgid ""
"When xonsh starts, it builds a long-lived **session** object that holds the "
"environment, history, command pipeline, parser, executor, jobs and so on. "
"Two Python objects expose this state:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:11
msgid ""
":class:`xonsh.built_ins.XonshSessionInterface` — a small, **stable** surface "
"intended for end users and scripts. Reachable through the ``@`` shortcut "
"(``@.env``, ``@.history``, ``@.imp``, ``@.lastcmd``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:15
msgid ""
":class:`xonsh.built_ins.XonshSession` — the **internal** session container "
"with everything xonsh needs at runtime. Reachable as ``__xonsh__`` (or "
"``xonsh.built_ins.XSH``). Useful when you are extending xonsh, writing "
"completers, hooks, or low-level integrations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:20
msgid "This page documents both, with a focus on day-to-day use cases."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:24
msgid "``XonshSessionInterface`` and the ``@`` shortcut"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:26
msgid ""
"``XonshSessionInterface`` is the **public, narrow** surface of a xonsh "
"session. It is exposed as a single-character shortcut: the ``@`` token."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:29
msgid ""
"Internally, the parser rewrites every ``@.<name>`` into ``__xonsh__."
"interface.<name>``, so the two are exactly equivalent. ``@`` on its own "
"evaluates to the interface object itself."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:33
msgid ""
"This means you can use ``@`` directly inside Python and subprocess mode:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:35
msgid ""
"# Print the value of $HOME via the typed Env API\n"
"echo @(@.env.get('HOME', '/tmp'))\n"
"\n"
"# Last exit code\n"
"echo \"exit was\" @(@.lastcmd.rtn)\n"
"\n"
"# Inline import + call in one expression\n"
"echo @(@.imp.json.dumps({'ok': True}))"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:47
msgid "``@.env`` — current environment"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:49
msgid ""
"``@.env`` is the current :class:`xonsh.environ.Env` instance. Use it any "
"time you want a programmatic, typed read or write of an environment "
"variable, instead of the shell-style ``$VAR`` form."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:53 ../../../xonsh_session.rst:79
#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:109 ../../../xonsh_session.rst:135
msgid "Use cases:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:55
msgid ""
"# Safe lookup with default\n"
"home = @.env.get('HOME', '/tmp')\n"
"\n"
"# Temporary override scoped to a `with`-block — restored on exit\n"
"with @.env.swap(EDITOR='vi', PAGER='cat'):\n"
"    $[git log --oneline -3]\n"
"\n"
"# Register a custom env variable with a validator and default\n"
"@.env.register('MY_TIMEOUT', type='int', default=30,\n"
"               doc='Request timeout in seconds.')"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:68
msgid ""
"``@.env`` shines when the variable name is **dynamic** (built from a string) "
"and you need default value, since ``$VAR`` requires a literal name in the "
"source."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:73
msgid "``@.history`` — history backend"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:75
msgid ""
"``@.history`` is the active :class:`xonsh.history.History` backend "
"(``json``, ``sqlite``, or ``dummy``). It exposes the same API regardless of "
"the configured backend."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:81
msgid ""
"# Last command typed in this session (raw input)\n"
"print(@.history.inps[-1])\n"
"\n"
"# Iterate session items: each is {'inp': cmd, 'ts': (start, end)}\n"
"for item in @.history.items():\n"
"    print(item['inp'])\n"
"\n"
"# All history (across sessions)\n"
"for item in @.history.all_items(newest_first=True):\n"
"    print(item['inp'])\n"
"    break\n"
"\n"
"# Force a flush to disk (same as `history flush`)\n"
"@.history.flush()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:98
msgid ""
"The ``history`` xonsh command is the user-facing wrapper around this object. "
"``@.history`` is what you reach for when you need data, not text."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:102
msgid "``@.imp`` — inline importer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:104
msgid ""
"``@.imp`` is an :class:`xonsh.built_ins.InlineImporter` instance. It turns "
"``@.imp.<module>`` into ``__import__('<module>')`` on first attribute "
"access, so you can pull in and use a stdlib (or third-party) module without "
"a separate ``import`` line."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:111
msgid ""
"# One-liners that would otherwise need an import\n"
"echo @(@.imp.time.time())\n"
"echo @(@.imp.json.dumps({'pid': @.imp.os.getpid()}))\n"
"echo @(@.imp.platform.system())\n"
"\n"
"# Pipe a Python value into a subprocess\n"
"echo @(@.imp.uuid.uuid4()) | tr - _\n"
"\n"
"# Handy for startup aliases — no upfront import needed\n"
"aliases['epoch'] = lambda args: print(int(@.imp.time.time()))"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:124
msgid ""
"For repeated use of the same module, a regular ``import`` is faster and "
"clearer; ``@.imp`` is for ad-hoc, one-shot calls where adding an ``import`` "
"would just be noise."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:129
msgid "``@.lastcmd`` — last command pipeline"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:131
msgid ""
"``@.lastcmd`` is the :class:`xonsh.procs.pipelines.CommandPipeline` of the "
"most recently completed subprocess-mode command. It is updated every time "
"you run a subprocess (``ls``, ``$(...)``, ``![...]``, etc.)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:137
msgid ""
"ls /nope\n"
"print('exit:', @.lastcmd.rtn)        # exit code\n"
"print('stdout:', @.lastcmd.output)   # captured stdout (if any)\n"
"print('stderr:', @.lastcmd.errors)   # captured stderr (if any)\n"
"print('args:',   @.lastcmd.args)     # the parsed command list\n"
"print('alias:',  @.lastcmd.alias)    # alias resolved (if any)\n"
"\n"
"# Build a \"fail fast\" wrapper in a script\n"
"git pull\n"
"if @.lastcmd.rtn != 0:\n"
"    raise SystemExit('git pull failed')"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:154
msgid "``XonshSession`` — the internal session"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:156
msgid ""
"``XonshSession`` is the *full* container that xonsh builds at startup. It is "
"reachable in two equivalent ways:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:159
msgid ""
"``__xonsh__`` — bound into ``builtins`` when the session loads, so every "
"executed scope sees it as a global."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:161
msgid "``xonsh.built_ins.XSH`` — the canonical module-level singleton."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:165
msgid ""
"Most attributes here are internal. They are exposed because xonsh needs them "
"to talk to itself, but they are **not** part of the stable API. Anything in :"
"class:`XonshSessionInterface` (the ``@`` shortcut) is meant to outlive "
"releases; everything else here may change. Prefer ``@.<x>`` or ``XSH."
"interface`` whenever an equivalent exists."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:172
msgid "Useful fields"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:174
msgid ""
"The following attributes are the most commonly used by hooks, completers, "
"and :doc:`xonsh RC <xonshrc>` automation. They are listed roughly in the "
"order you are likely to need them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:178
msgid ""
"``__xonsh__.env`` — same :class:`xonsh.environ.Env` as ``@.env``; populated "
"by :meth:`XonshSession.load`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:181
msgid "``__xonsh__.history`` — same as ``@.history``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:183
msgid ""
"``__xonsh__.aliases`` — the live :class:`xonsh.aliases.Aliases` mapping. "
"Setting ``__xonsh__.aliases['ll'] = 'ls -la'`` from your :doc:`xonsh RC "
"<xonshrc>` is the canonical way to define an alias. The property delegates "
"to ``commands_cache.aliases`` and is read-only on the session itself; mutate "
"the mapping in place."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:189
msgid ""
"``__xonsh__.completers`` — ``OrderedDict`` of registered completer "
"callables. Lazily initialized on first access. Modify with the ``completer`` "
"command or by mutating the dict directly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:193
msgid ""
"``__xonsh__.shell`` — the active shell wrapper (:class:`xonsh.shell.Shell`). "
"The concrete implementation is in ``__xonsh__.shell.shell`` "
"(``PromptToolkitShell``, ``ReadlineShell`` or ``DumbShell``). Use this when "
"you need to call a shell-specific method like ``print_color``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:199
msgid ""
"``__xonsh__.execer`` — the :class:`xonsh.execer.Execer`, which parses, "
"compiles, and runs xonsh source. Use ``execer.exec(src)`` / ``execer."
"eval(src)`` to evaluate xonsh code from inside Python."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:203
msgid ""
"``__xonsh__.builtins`` — a ``SimpleNamespace`` containing the xonsh-injected "
"builtins: ``XonshError``, ``XonshCalledProcessError``, ``evalx``, ``execx``, "
"``compilex``, ``events``, ``print_color``, ``printx``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:208
msgid ""
"``__xonsh__.ctx`` — the global Python context dict that all xonsh user code "
"runs against. Equivalent to ``globals()`` for the REPL. Useful for "
"completers and hooks that want to inspect what the user has defined."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:213
msgid ""
"``__xonsh__.all_jobs`` — dict of background jobs keyed by job id. See "
"``jobs``, ``fg``, ``bg``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:216
msgid "``__xonsh__.lastcmd`` — same as ``@.lastcmd``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:218
msgid ""
"``__xonsh__.sessionid`` — UUID of the current session, used in history "
"filenames and similar."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:221
msgid ""
"``__xonsh__.rc_files`` — list of :doc:`xonsh RC <xonshrc>` files actually "
"loaded."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:223
msgid ""
"``__xonsh__.builtins.events`` — the :class:`xonsh.events.events` registry; "
"how you subscribe to ``on_postcommand``, ``on_pre_prompt``, ``on_chdir`` and "
"friends."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:227
msgid ""
"``__xonsh__.exit`` — set this to an integer from anywhere to ask xonsh to "
"exit on the next loop iteration with that return code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:232
msgid "Lifecycle and helpers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:234
msgid ""
"These are the methods you call when *embedding* xonsh, hot-reloading the "
"session in tests, or writing tooling that wraps it:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:237
msgid ""
"``load(execer=None, ctx=None, inherit_env=True, **kwargs)`` — fully "
"initialize the session: build the env, install builtins into Python, hook "
"``atexit`` for history flush, install signal handlers. Called by ``main."
"main_xonsh`` once at startup."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:241
msgid ""
"``unload()`` — reverse of ``load``: undo env replacement, restore Python's "
"``exit``/``quit``, flush history, remove ``__xonsh__`` from builtins."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:244
msgid ""
"``link_builtins()`` / ``unlink_builtins()`` — rebind the proxy builtins "
"(``XonshError``, ``events``, etc.) into Python's ``builtins`` namespace. "
"Used internally by ``load``/``unload``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:247
msgid ""
"``cmd(*args, **kwargs)`` — return a :class:`xonsh.built_ins.Cmd` *builder* "
"that lets you compose a subprocess pipeline programmatically before "
"dispatching it. See the next section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:252
msgid ""
"Subprocess dispatchers — how ``$()``, ``!()``, ``$[]``, ``![]`` map to Python"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_session.rst:254
msgid ""
"Every subprocess-mode operator in xonsh is a thin parser sugar around a "
"``XonshSession.subproc_*`` method. Knowing the mapping is what lets you "
"*construct commands programmatically* (for example from a list of arguments) "
"and still get exactly the same semantics as the literal form."
msgstr ""

